      "content": "I understand your concerns. First, let's calculate the volume of the shell without considering it being hollow. For a moon with a uniform density, its approximate radius is about 1737.5 km, so let's calculate the volume V, and essentially then the mass M if the moon were solid:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (1737.5 \\times 10^5 \\, \\text{cm})^3 \\]\n\\[ V = 2.197 \\times 10^{23} \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\]\n\nGiven the average density of the lunar crust is 3.3 g/cm^3, if it were a solid body, the mass would be:\n\n\\[ M_{\\text{solid}} = V \\cdot \\text{density} = 2.197 \\times 10^{23} \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\times 3.3 \\, \\text{g/cm}^3 = 7.2301 \\times 10^{22} \\, \\text{g} \\]\n\\[ M_{\\text{solid}} = 7,240,",
